  <h2 id="learn">How to learn banking and insurance interview answers cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Banking and insurance interview answers is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free YouTube mock interviews.</li>
    <li>Free public annual reports and employer pages.</li>
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM finance lessons.</li>
    <li>A senior in the sector.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    This week, write your four-part answer and say it aloud to one person.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="structure">A four-part answer structure</h2>

  <p>
    Interviewers hear many scripted answers. A simple, honest structure stands out.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Part</th>
          <th>What to say</th>
          <th>Example prompt</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Part"><strong>Reason</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What to say">One honest reason you like the field</td>
          <td data-label="Example prompt">What drew you to money, people or rules?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Part"><strong>Proof</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What to say">One small thing you did that fits</td>
          <td data-label="Example prompt">A project, course or conversation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Part"><strong>Fit</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What to say">Why this employer and this role</td>
          <td data-label="Example prompt">What do you know about them?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Part"><strong>Learning</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What to say">What you hope to learn first</td>
          <td data-label="Example prompt">A skill or product area</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Practise it aloud in under ninety seconds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="avoid">Answers that weaken your case</h2>

  <p>
    Some lines sound safe but give the interviewer nothing to remember.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Weak line</th>
          <th>Why it fails</th>
          <th>Better approach</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
